 \subsection{Harness: Directives And Cache State}\label{sec:speculation-directives}
 % \proposed[both paragraphs of Speculation Directives (``The simulator must be made to actually realize\ldots''); in gem5 Hooks, ``Three source control hooks\ldots'' through ``\ldots{} over $\{0, 150\}$ cycles.'', ``Finally, for data-flow speculation\ldots'', the first sentence of ``Realizing these directives\ldots'' and ``Detecting transmission requires\ldots''; the timing paragraph ``We also sweep the resolution time\ldots'']{
 % [before pervasiveness review E3, E4, E6, 2026-11-16] The harness's hooks sit in gem5's branch predictor and out-of-order pipeline. The annotation gives each element's PC, each branch's mispredict mode and BTB target, the transmitter's kind, and for store bypass the (store, load) pair whose load must mispredict no alias. At each annotated branch, the harness overrides the predictor, forcing an unresolved branch to mispredict and a resolved one to predict correctly. The run configuration gives a grid of timing settings: each test draws one setting, and a sweep runs every setting and reports a hit if any setting triggers one. The harness holds each unresolved branch's resolution for 2{,}500, 5{,}000, or 10{,}000 cycles, so that the branch is still unresolved when the transmitter acts, and delays each resolved branch's resolution by 0, 500, or 2{,}500 cycles after its direction and target are known (Table~\ref{tab:instrtable}, rows~1--2), so that tests vary when resolved branches resolve, as they would if their conditions arrived late. 
